@node	0.8, Chapter 1, 0.7, Chapter 0
@section Reading this book

This section covers the interaction convention, how exercises map to SICP's
numbers, the signpost sentences, and the exercise map. It is the last
primer section; the convention it fixes applies from the first listing of
@ref{0.2} and never changes afterward.

@heading The interaction convention

Each interaction in the book is one @code{@@example kotlin} block: the call,
rendered in Kotlin, followed by its result as a comment line carrying a
@code{=>} marker.

@example kotlin
5L + 3L + 4L
// => 12

val size = 2L
5L + 3L + size
// => 10
@end example

@noindent
The marker keeps every result a valid comment in the source and readable in
the unhighlighted PDF, and the same value is asserted in the listing's file
under @file{kotlin/examples/}, so the transcript cannot drift from the code.
Lines the book's programs print rather than evaluate, a probe or a table
driven by @code{for}-each, appear as comment lines without the marker. Where
a chapter preserves a REPL session verbatim, the session's own responses
are kept in the same comment form, including the sessions' definitions:

@example kotlin
val a = 3L
// ;Value: a

val b = a + 1L
// ;Value: b

a + b + (a * b)
// ;Value: 19
@end example

@noindent
From chapter 4 on, where the book's programs are values of the runtime's
@code{Value} type, a transcript shows the same shape with the runtime's
printed forms. Nothing in a transcript is hand-copied: every result in
every listing is asserted by the file the listing names.

@heading How exercises map to SICP's numbers

Exercise numbers are one to one with SICP. An exercise whose material
survives in Kotlin keeps its number and its intent; only its language
changes. When a statement exists to teach Scheme itself, the edition
replaces it under the same number and says so. Each exercise row in the
edition's map carries one of three classes: @code{T} for a translation
where only the syntax changes, @code{A} for an adaptation where the idea
survives and the statement changes, and @code{R} for a replacement that
keeps the number but teaches the section's material on host terms. The map
is the file @file{docs/exercise-map.md} at the repository root, one table
per section.

Two fixed sentences appear in the text wherever the numbering needs
explaining, and they always read exactly as follows. A replacement says:

@quotation
@emph{SICP exercise @var{N.M} is about Scheme itself, so this edition
replaces it; the exercise below keeps the number and teaches the same
section material.}
@end quotation

@noindent
and an addition, numbered as the exercise it extends plus a lowercase
letter, says:

@quotation
@emph{Exercise @var{N.Ma} is added by this edition and extends exercise
@var{N.M}; SICP numbers stop at @var{N.M}.}
@end quotation

@noindent
At most one such addition exists per section per edition. The primer's own
exercises, 0.1 through 0.5, are this edition's additions in the same
spirit: they use only what @ref{0.2} through @ref{0.7} have taught, and
each one lives in the section that teaches its material, which is why the
sequence probe of exercise 0.5 sits in @ref{0.3} rather than at the end of
the chapter.

@heading A worked example

The three sessions of exercise 0.1 become Kotlin transcripts of the
convention's shape. The arithmetic session, evaluated in order:

@example kotlin
486L
// => 486

100L
// => 100

5L + 3L + 4L
// => 12

10L - 9L
// => 1

2L * 4L + (4L - 6L)
// => 6
@end example

@noindent
and the @code{square} session of that exercise, which needs a definition
first:

@example kotlin
fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x

square(21L)
// => 441

square(2L + 5L)
// => 49

square(square(3L))
// => 81
@end example

@noindent
The full transcript, computed and asserted, is
@file{kotlin/examples/ch0/S0_8Transcripts.kt}. With the convention, the
numbering, and the signposts in hand, chapter 1 begins: @ref{1.1} builds
abstractions with procedures, and from its first listing every construct
will be one this primer has already shown.

